module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.AtPrime.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.RingHom.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Spectrum.Prime.Defs

/-!
# Compatible surjective local maps from a common prime localization

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tags 00CP, 00E9, 07BI and 07BJ, with the common-prime
and fraction-preimage argument of the DAG node
(blueprint: lem-trf-common-localization-surjective-diagram).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe uD uP uQ uA

namespace Localization

/-- Compatible fraction-generating maps to local rings factor uniquely through their
common prime localization, giving a diagram of surjective local ring maps.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 00CP (universal property and fraction formula),
Tags 00E9, 07BI and 07BJ (units, prime localizations and local maps), with the explicit
common-prime refinement (blueprint: lem-trf-common-localization-surjective-diagram).

Proof: First a local map has inverse image of the target maximal ideal equal to the
source maximal ideal: locality gives one inclusion, and an element outside the source
maximal ideal is a unit, whose image is a unit. Apply this to both quotient maps and
use the equality of their composites. The resulting common inverse image is prime:
it is proper, and if a product belongs to it while the first factor does not, the
image of that factor is a unit, so the image of the second factor is in the maximal
ideal. These arguments use lem-weil-pbc-local-ring-unit-criterion.
Next, every element outside this prime maps to a unit in each target. Apply
lem-weil-pbc-ring-localization-universal-property to all three maps, obtaining the
extensions, their uniqueness and the three fraction formulas. Uniqueness also shows
that the composites with the quotient maps equal the third extension.
For surjectivity onto P, write p*f(t)=f(a) with f(t) a unit. The common-prime equality
implies t lies outside the prime, and a/t maps to p by the fraction formula. Repeat
for Q. Lift an element of A along the surjection from P and then along the first
extension to obtain surjectivity onto A. Finally, the maximal ideal of the prime
localization is generated by the images of elements of the prime, by
def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime. Each extension sends these generators into its
target maximal ideal, hence sends every finite sum of their multiples into that
ideal. Thus all three extensions are local. -/
theorem exists_commonLocalization_surjective_diagram
    {D : Type uD} {P : Type uP} {Q : Type uQ} {A : Type uA}
    [CommRing D] [CommRing P] [CommRing Q] [CommRing A]
    [IsLocalRing P] [IsLocalRing Q] [IsLocalRing A]
    (u : P →+* A) (v : Q →+* A) [IsLocalHom u] [IsLocalHom v]
    (hu : Function.Surjective u) (hv : Function.Surjective v)
    (f : D →+* P) (g : D →+* Q) (hcomm : u.comp f = v.comp g)
    (hf : ∀ p : P, ∃ a t : D, IsUnit (f t) ∧ p * f t = f a)
    (hg : ∀ q : Q, ∃ c z : D, IsUnit (g z) ∧ q * g z = g c) :
    ∃ n : PrimeSpectrum D,
      n.asIdeal = (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A).comap (u.comp f) ∧
      n.asIdeal = (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal P).comap f ∧
      n.asIdeal = (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal Q).comap g ∧
      ∃ (α : Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal →+* P)
        (β : Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal →+* Q)
        (γ : Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal →+* A),
        α.comp (algebraMap D (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal)) = f ∧
        β.comp (algebraMap D (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal)) = g ∧
        γ.comp (algebraMap D (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal)) = u.comp f ∧
        Function.Surjective α ∧ Function.Surjective β ∧ Function.Surjective γ ∧
        IsLocalHom α ∧ IsLocalHom β ∧ IsLocalHom γ ∧
        u.comp α = γ ∧ v.comp β = γ ∧
        (∀ α' : Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal →+* P,
          α'.comp (algebraMap D (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal)) = f → α' = α) ∧
        (∀ β' : Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal →+* Q,
          β'.comp (algebraMap D (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal)) = g → β' = β) ∧
        (∀ γ' : Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal →+* A,
          γ'.comp (algebraMap D (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal)) = u.comp f → γ' = γ) ∧
        (∀ (d : D) (t : n.asIdeal.primeCompl),
          α (IsLocalization.mk' (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal) d t) =
            f d * Ring.inverse (f t)) ∧
        (∀ (d : D) (t : n.asIdeal.primeCompl),
          β (IsLocalization.mk' (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal) d t) =
            g d * Ring.inverse (g t)) ∧
        (∀ (d : D) (t : n.asIdeal.primeCompl),
          γ (IsLocalization.mk' (Localization.AtPrime n.asIdeal) d t) =
            (u.comp f) d * Ring.inverse ((u.comp f) t)) := by
  sorry

end Localization
